################## Project Struture ##########################
"""
Seperate out routes based on thier functionality :

Project_Backend/
├── main.py
├── config.py
├── database.py
├── models.py
├── schemas.py
│
├── routes/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   └── users.py
│
├── tests/
│   └── test_main.py
│
└── .env

What is APIRouter?

This is the key concept.

Normally you have:

app = FastAPI()

# @app.get("/users")
# def get_users():
#     ...

# With APIRouter, we create a group of routes:

# from fastapi import APIRouter

# router = APIRouter()

# @router.get("/users")
# def get_users():
#     ...

# The router isn't the FastAPI application itself.

Think of it as:

FastAPI application
       │
       ├── users router
       │      ├── GET /users
       │      ├── GET /users/{id}
       │      ├── POST /users
       │      └── DELETE /users/{id}
       │
       └── auth router
              └── POST /login

Then main.py connects those routers to the application.

This is the architectural benefit:

Before
main.py
│
├── POST /users
├── GET /users
├── GET /users/{id}
├── PUT /users/{id}
├── PATCH /users/{id}
├── DELETE /users/{id}
├── POST /login
├── GET /protected
├── GET /admin/dashboard
├── middleware
├── authentication
├── database
├── models
└── external APIs

After this refactor
main.py
│
├── create FastAPI app
├── configure application
└── include routers

database.py
└── database

models.py
└── database models

schemas.py
└── API schemas

routes/
└── users.py
    ├── POST /users
    ├── GET /users
    ├── GET /users/{id}
    ├── PUT /users/{id}
    └── PATCH /users/{id}

That's already a much more maintainable application.

First: what is a route?

You already know this:

@app.get("/users")
def get_users():
    ...

This means:

"When somebody sends a GET request to /users, execute get_users()."

So:

GET /users
      ↓
get_users()

The @app.get() is attaching the endpoint directly to the FastAPI application.

2. What is the problem?

Imagine your application eventually has:

100 endpoints

If we put all 100 here:

app = FastAPI()

@app.get(...)
...

@app.post(...)
...

@app.put(...)
...

@app.delete(...)
...

# 100 endpoints...

main.py becomes enormous.

So we say:

"Let's group related endpoints into separate files."

For example:

users.py
    ↓
all user-related endpoints

auth.py
    ↓
all authentication-related endpoints

products.py
    ↓
all product-related endpoints
3. What is APIRouter?

This is the only new concept you need right now.

Normally:

app = FastAPI()

creates the main application.

Similarly:

router = APIRouter()

creates a mini collection of routes.

Think of it like this:

FastAPI application
        │
        ├── User Router
        │
        ├── Auth Router
        │
        └── Product Router

The router is not another server.

It is simply a way of grouping endpoints.


But how does FastAPI know about this router?

This is where main.py comes in.

main.py:

from fastapi import FastAPI

from routes.users import router as users_router

app = FastAPI()

app.include_router(users_router)
"""